PR #155  overall: potential_risk
Spec: HWCFG2_en/HWCFG3_en changed from W1SS to R: software that writes these assuming writable feature enables would silently operate without expected IOPMP protection, enabling privilege escape via unchecked transactions.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu.c:543-576  kvm_riscv_vcpu_setup_config
      Sets henvcfg feature-enable bits (PBMTE, STCE, ADUE, CBZE, CBCFE, CBIE) based solely on ISA string extensions without any WARL read-back verification. If hardware implements any of these bits as read-only—contrary to the ISA string—the hypervisor proceeds assuming features are active. The erroneous config is later committed to hardware in kvm_arch_vcpu_load (line 596/615) without verification. This is the exact write-then-trust pattern: the ISA string is treated as an authoritative source of CSR writability rather than as a hint requiring hardware confirmation.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu.c:596-623  kvm_arch_vcpu_load
      Commits cfg->henvcfg to CSR_HENVCFG via csr_write (or nacl_csr_write) on every vCPU load without reading back the CSR to verify which bits actually took effect. This is the precise point where 'assumed active' feature-enable bits get pushed to hardware. If any bit in cfg->henvcfg is WARL-read-only-zero on this hardware, the write silently fails and the guest runs without the expected security enforcement (e.g., no ADUE for Svadu, no PBMTE for Svpbmt). The kernel has no detection mechanism for this mismatch.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_onereg.c:162-167  kvm_riscv_vcpu_isa_enable_allowed
      Explicitly documents and checks the parent-child CSR read-only dependency for SVADU: henvcfg.ADUE is read-only zero if menvcfg.ADUE is zero. The guard arch_has_hw_pte_young() verifies the host has actually enabled Svadu before allowing the guest to enable it. This is correct defensive coding—the code does not blindly trust that setting ADUE will work; it gates on the host-level capability that makes the bit writable.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_onereg.c:250-255  kvm_riscv_vcpu_isa_disable_allowed
      Mirrors the SVADE disable dependency with the same explicit documentation and guard. The gate arch_has_hw_pte_young() correctly prevents disabling Svade (which would require writing ADUE) when the host hasn't enabled Svadu, since ADUE would be read-only zero. This acknowledges the conditionally-read-only CSR field pattern and correctly gates the operation.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/usercfi.c:62-75  set_shstk_status
      Writes ENVCFG_SSE to CSR_ENVCFG via csr_write without any read-back verification. The code assumes zicfiss support (checked via is_user_shstk_enabled()) makes SSE unconditionally writable, but if hardware implements SSE as WARL and silently ignores the write, shadow-stack enforcement appears active (ubcfi_en=1, envcfg bit set in thread struct) but the hardware does not enforce it. This is the exact IOPMP HWCFG2_en write-then-trust pattern—software state diverges from hardware state with no detection.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/usercfi.c:92-105  set_indir_lp_status
      Writes ENVCFG_LPE to CSR_ENVCFG via csr_write without read-back verification. Same pattern as set_shstk_status: zicfilp support (is_user_lpad_enabled()) is assumed to make LPE writable, but no hardware confirmation. If LPE is read-only-zero, indirect-landing-pad enforcement is silently absent while the kernel believes it is active. The ufcfi_en state variable and envcfg bit track software intent but not hardware reality.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/process.c:423-441  tagged_addr_init
      THE GOOD PATTERN. Explicitly acknowledges envcfg.PMM is a WARL field ('envcfg.PMM is a WARL field. Detect which values are supported.'), clears the field first, then probes each PMLEN value via try_to_set_pmm() which does csr_set + csr_read_clear to confirm the write actually took effect. This is the correct defensive approach the kernel should apply uniformly to all envcfg/henvcfg feature-enable fields. The contrast with other call sites shows the kernel is aware of WARL risks but applies verification inconsistently.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_sbi_fwft.c:156-193  kvm_sbi_fwft_set_pointer_masking_pmlen
      On line 190, writes henvcfg.PMM via csr_write(CSR_HENVCFG, ...) without reading back to confirm the write took effect. Although the function guards against unsupported PMLEN values via the have_vs_pmlen_* flags (probed once at init), it does not re-verify at write time. If HENVCFG.PMM becomes conditionally read-only after the probe (e.g., due to a concurrent menvcfg change or a hardware state transition), the write silently fails and the VCPU runs with a different PMM configuration than expected—same 'assume write succeeded' pattern.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_sbi_fwft.c:138-149  kvm_sbi_fwft_pointer_masking_pmlen_supported
      Probes HENVCFG.PMM writability via try_to_set_pmm() at VCPU init time and caches the result in fwft->have_vs_pmlen_*. The cached probe result is then trusted for the lifetime of the VCPU. If HENVCFG.PMM bits were to become conditionally-DC (e.g., dependent on another CSR field changing at runtime), the stale cache would cause kvm_sbi_fwft_set_pointer_masking_pmlen to either reject valid PMLEN values or accept now-invalid ones. This is analogous to caching HWCFG2_en writability probe before HWCFG3_en is changed.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:214-231  (macro definitions)
      These are static bit-mask constant definitions for ENVCFG fields. They are just integer constants—there is no runtime behavior to be compliant or non-compliant. The vulnerability lies not in the macro definitions themselves but in the call sites that use these macros with csr_write() without WARL read-back. The macros are correctly defined to match the ISA specification. The risk that 'if any of these bits change R/W attribute in a spec revision, all call sites would silently break' is a valid concern, but it is a property of the call sites, not of these constant definitions.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/switch_to.h:73-82  envcfg_update_bits
      Context-switch-time csr_write(CSR_ENVCFG, envcfg) that assumes all envcfg bits in the in-memory thread.envcfg are writable and take effect. No WARL read-back. If any bit is actually read-only on this hardware, the task runs with an incorrect security posture (e.g., SSE, LPE, PMM silently not applied) while the kernel's in-memory state claims they are active. This is a persistent divergence until the next envcfg_update_bits call—which could be the entire lifetime of the task.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/switch_to.h:84-89  __switch_to_envcfg
      ALTERNATIVE-patched csrw CSR_ENVCFG at every context switch via switch_to macro (line 124). Same write-and-trust vulnerability as envcfg_update_bits, but this path executes on every context switch with no possibility of verification due to the performance-critical inline asm. If the R/W attribute of any ENVCFG bit changes in a spec revision (analogous to the HWCFG2_en/HWCFG3_en R/W correction), every context switch silently fails to apply the intended security configuration. The ALTERNATIVE mechanism selects between nop and csrw based on extension presence but never validates the write effect.

